Routine implementation decisions will be made collaboratively by the project team, with the team member responsible for the relevant component leading the discussion.
Decisions affecting the overall project scope, architecture, AI behaviour, privacy, access control or major deliverables will require agreement from the project team.
Examples include:
adding or removing functionality from the MVP;
changing the selected retrieval approach;
changing the database or application architecture;
changing how project-specific information is provided to Agentaus;
changing role-access rules;
changing evaluation measures; and
introducing functionality currently identified as future development.
Where a decision may significantly affect sponsor expectations or academic requirements, guidance will be obtained from the relevant supervisor, mentor or sponsor.

The team will use short development iterations during the 10-week project period. Progress will be reviewed regularly against the agreed scope, requirements, milestones and deliverables.
During project reviews, the team will consider:
activities completed;
activities currently in progress;
upcoming activities;
technical blockers;
Agentaus API or integration issues;
retrieval and grounding issues;
identified risks;
testing results; and
changes required for the next iteration.
Prototype demonstrations and stakeholder feedback will be used to progressively validate requirements and refine the system.
Significant issues affecting project delivery will be communicated to the academic supervisor/mentor and sponsor where appropriate.

Scope control is particularly important because the project must deliver a functional prototype within ten weeks.
Proposed changes will be assessed according to:
relevance to the identified problem;
value to the core Capstone Assistant;
estimated development effort;
technical complexity;
impact on existing functionality;
additional testing requirements; and
impact on the delivery schedule.
Priority will be given to completing the agreed Micro-SaaS functionality before introducing additional features.
Functionality such as comprehensive task management, advanced scheduling, automated Gantt charts, risk-management workflows, progress prediction, LMS integration and enterprise deployment will remain outside the current project unless formally reconsidered and sufficient time is available.

As the system uses Agentaus AI to generate natural-language responses, AI behaviour will form an important part of project governance.
The assistant will operate using a human-in-the-loop approach. AI-generated responses will provide assistance but will not replace formal academic advice, mentor guidance or stakeholder judgement.
The team will apply the following controls:
project-specific information will only be retrieved for authorised users;
relevant retrieved information will be supplied to Agentaus to support grounded responses;
prompts will be controlled and versioned;
insufficient information will trigger fallback behaviour rather than intentional fabrication;
selected AI responses will be evaluated for relevance and grounded accuracy;
hallucination occurrence will be recorded during evaluation;
AI response latency and request failures will be monitored;
unnecessary project or user information will be minimised in monitoring data; and
users will be able to provide simple feedback on AI responses.
The Administrator dashboard will provide summarised information about AI and system performance so that issues can be identified during prototype development and evaluation.

Issues will initially be discussed within the four-member project team.
An issue should be escalated when it:
blocks development of another component;
affects a major project milestone;
requires a significant change to the agreed scope;
creates an access-control or privacy concern;
causes repeated Agentaus API failures;
significantly affects AI response quality;
cannot be resolved within the project team; or
threatens delivery of the MVP within the 10-week timeframe.
The general escalation path will be:
Responsible Team Member → Four-Member Project Team → Academic Supervisor/Mentor → Project Sponsor where relevant
Technical issues will normally be resolved within the team where possible, while academic, scope and sponsor-related issues will be escalated to the appropriate stakeholder.
